#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Persist INDmoney (INDstocks) order updates from the Redis Stream `indmoney:order-updates:stream` into
`indmoney.order_updates`, and keep persisting until stopped.

    bin/indmoney/orders/store_orders_to_db
    redis-cli XINFO GROUPS indmoney:order-updates:stream  # lag and pending count

`bin/indmoney/orders/websocket_order_details` appends every order update to the stream, each as `{"timestamp": ..., "data": <INDstocks'
order>}`; this reads it as the `persist` consumer group and writes one row per update - every transition of an
order, not only its latest state - to the `indmoney.order_updates` hypertable with COPY, a batch at a time.

**Nothing is lost across a restart.** An entry is acknowledged only after the batch holding it has been
committed; a crash, a refused write or a lost connection leaves it pending, and pending entries are read first
at the next start or after the failure. A batch committed just before a crash and not yet acknowledged is
written a second time. The stream is capped at about 20,000 entries, so updates trimmed while this is stopped
are lost and counted as skipped. Run one instance.

**Columns.** INDstocks' order fields sit at the top level of its message (or inside a nested `data` object, which
`bin/indmoney/orders/websocket_order_details` has already unwrapped), and INDstocks documents only a handful of them - `order_id`,
`order_status`, `filled_quantity`, `remaining_quantity`, `average_price` and a millisecond `timestamp` - so each
column is read under the spellings the field plausibly uses, the first present one winning, and the order is kept
whole in `raw`:

| column | from INDstocks, first present |
| --- | --- |
| `time` | when `bin/indmoney/orders/websocket_order_details` received it |
| `order_id`, `exchange_order_id`, `parent_order_id` | `order_id`/`orderId`, `exchange_order_id`/`exch_order_id`, `parent_order_id` |
| `status`, `status_message` | `order_status`/`status` on the shared vocabulary, `status_message`/`rejection_reason`/`message`/`remarks` |
| `id`, `instrument_token`, `tradingsymbol`, `exchange` | `exchange:trading_symbol`, `security_id`/`token`/`instrument_token`, `trading_symbol`/`tradingsymbol`/`symbol`, `exchange`/`exchange_segment`/`segment` |
| `transaction_type`, `product`, `order_type`, `validity` | `txn_type`/`transaction_type`/`side`, `product`/`product_type`, `order_type`/`price_type`, `validity`/`time_in_force`, on the shared vocabulary |
| quantities | `quantity`/`qty`/`order_quantity`, `filled_quantity`/`filled_qty`/`executed_quantity`, `remaining_quantity`/`pending_quantity`/`unfilled_quantity` (or quantity less filled), `cancelled_quantity`, `disclosed_quantity` |
| prices | `price`/`limit_price`, `trigger_price`/`stop_price`, `average_price`/`avg_price` |
| `order_timestamp`, `exchange_timestamp` | `order_timestamp`/`timestamp`, `exchange_timestamp`/`timestamp` |
| `tag`, `raw` | `tag`/`remarks`/`algo_id`, the whole order |

A timestamp is an epoch - milliseconds when above 10^11, as INDstocks sends it, seconds otherwise - which is
timezone-free; a timestamp that arrives as text instead is read as IST wall clock. The shared vocabulary maps each
broker's spelling of a status, side, product, order type and validity onto one set - `PENDING`, `OPEN`,
`COMPLETE`, `CANCELLED`, `REJECTED`, `EXPIRED` for status - and passes an unknown term through uppercased, so it
shows up rather than disappearing. Blanks and `NA` are stored as NULL. An update without an order id, or one that
is not JSON, is skipped and acknowledged.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, after writing and acknowledging the batch in hand. A database or
Redis that cannot be reached is retried with backoff rather than ending the process.

**The table** is created on start from `080_indmoney_streams.sql` in `stock_brokers/instruments/ticks/utilities/sql/ddl`,
whose statements are all safe to run again, so a new database needs no separate step. A start that cannot apply
it exits 1.
"""

import io
import sys
import json
import time
import signal
import argparse
import threading
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo

